---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Virtual slime is a blob you poke and pull in the browser. Press, drag, let go. Stiffness
      follows Feel intensity. If a finger leaves the canvas without a pointerup, Release appears
      so the blob does not stay glued to a ghost.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a grab on a square stage. Output: a stretch that eases back, with no
      goo skins and no download.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Slime →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What is virtual slime? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Physical slime is stretch and squish. Browser slime is one blob, not a store of colours.
      Mobile apps bury the blob behind in-app purchases. Flash-era toys are gone. This page is
      the gesture: grab a vertex, pull, release.
    </p>
    <p>
      The fill is mixed from the accent and the surface, so the blob does not vanish on a light
      theme. That contrast failure is common on other slime pages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does virtual slime work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The blob is a ring of points. A grab pins one point to the pointer. The others follow with
      a stiffness from Feel intensity. Let go and the ring eases toward a circle. Therefore a
      pull feels stretchy without a mesh zoo.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, low intensity is calm. High is stretchy. However, reduced motion only stops
      idle jiggle. A grab still stretches, because that is the task.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="release">
    <h2>What does Release do? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A pointer that leaves the canvas sometimes never fires pointerup. Other toys keep the grab.
      The blob then follows nothing. This page listens for pointerup, pointercancel, and
      lostpointercapture. If a grab is still held with no buttons down, Release shows. One tap
      detaches. It stays hidden when the path is clean.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Silent until it is stuck">
      <p>
        Recovery that is always on screen is clutter. Release exists for the missed pointerup, and
        for that only.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reloading a stuck blob">
      <p>
        A refresh works and costs the session. Release is the one-tap detach the failure actually
        needs.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Reloading a stuck blob works and costs the session. Release is the one-tap detach. Looking
      for colour skins is the mobile-app habit this page will not feed. One blob is the product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Expecting idle jiggle under reduced motion is the other miss. The grab still works.
      Therefore the fidget survives a motion setting that other toys treat as a hard off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="vs">
    <h2>Slime vs alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Mobile slime apps bury the blob behind in-app goo skins. Kinetic sand is a pile you drag.
      A spinner is a flick. This page is pull. Pick slime when you want stretch in the tab, with
      a Release control if the pointer leaves the canvas.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="feel">
    <h2>How do I make slime calmer or stretchier? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Settings → Feel intensity maps to stiffness. Low is calm. High is stretchy. There is no
      second slider on the page, because every fidget already shares that preference. Reduced
      motion stops idle jiggle; a grab still stretches, because that is the task.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the blob <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, pull the blob, let go, and use Play if you want it to fill the screen. If it
      ever sticks, tap Release.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Slime →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
